WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.689
Saturday, noon, a full restaurant.

2
00:00:04.689 --> 00:00:09.018
A new menu is ready, and the waiters trained for it wait at the back door.

3
00:00:09.018 --> 00:00:12.469
How do you swap the whole shift while guests keep arriving?

4
00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:13.184
Plan A.

5
00:00:13.184 --> 00:00:14.430
Close for an hour.

6
00:00:14.430 --> 00:00:17.822
Send the old shift home, then let the new one in.

7
00:00:17.822 --> 00:00:18.306
Simple.

8
00:00:18.306 --> 00:00:21.075
And the street would remember that hour.

9
00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:22.432
Plan B.

10
00:00:22.432 --> 00:00:25.016
Swap everyone at once, no closing.

11
00:00:25.016 --> 00:00:25.396
Bold.

12
00:00:25.396 --> 00:00:31.551
But if the new waiters cannot find the plates, every table notices, all together.

13
00:00:31.551 --> 00:00:33.603
Bold is not a release plan.

14
00:00:33.833 --> 00:00:36.107
So the point is: we need a third way.

15
00:00:36.107 --> 00:00:40.225
Change the shift a little at a time, and never leave a table alone.

16
00:00:40.933 --> 00:00:45.122
Kubernetes has a shift manager for this, the Deployment.

17
00:00:45.122 --> 00:00:49.011
Ours runs hello-devops, three copies, version 1.0.0.

18
00:00:49.233 --> 00:00:51.026
It does not make Pods itself.

19
00:00:51.026 --> 00:00:58.505
It keeps a shift list for each version, called a ReplicaSet, and the ReplicaSet keeps three Pods of that version running.

20
00:00:58.733 --> 00:01:03.028
In front of them, a Service sends visitors to every Pod that is ready.

21
00:01:04.333 --> 00:01:06.683
Now you change the image to 1.1.0.

22
00:01:06.683 --> 00:01:14.907
That line is part of the Pod template, the recipe for each Pod, and only a change to the template starts a new version.

23
00:01:15.133 --> 00:01:22.756
So the Deployment opens a second shift list, a new ReplicaSet for 1.1.0, starting at zero Pods.

24
00:01:22.967 --> 00:01:25.745
A careful manager changes shifts like this.

25
00:01:25.745 --> 00:01:27.295
One new waiter comes in.

26
00:01:27.295 --> 00:01:30.267
When they are ready, one old waiter goes home.

27
00:01:30.267 --> 00:01:32.593
Repeat until the whole floor is new.

28
00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:34.395
Two dials set the pace.

29
00:01:34.395 --> 00:01:38.835
maxSurge: how many extra Pods may exist above the wanted number.

30
00:01:38.835 --> 00:01:42.234
maxUnavailable: how many may be missing below it.

31
00:01:42.234 --> 00:01:44.176
The lab picks one, and zero.

32
00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:47.591
Three replicas, one extra allowed, none missing.

33
00:01:47.591 --> 00:01:50.516
So during the update: at most how many Pods?

34
00:01:50.516 --> 00:01:52.377
And at least how many ready?

35
00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:56.138
At most four.

36
00:01:56.138 --> 00:01:57.816
At least three ready.

37
00:01:57.816 --> 00:01:59.493
Let us watch it hold.

38
00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:00.401
Step one.

39
00:02:00.401 --> 00:02:02.972
The new ReplicaSet scales to one.

40
00:02:02.972 --> 00:02:05.309
Four Pods: three old, one new.

41
00:02:05.533 --> 00:02:09.843
It is starting, but not ready yet, so the Service sends it nothing.

42
00:02:09.843 --> 00:02:11.451
And no old Pod may leave.

43
00:02:11.451 --> 00:02:13.703
Everyone waits for one word: ready.

44
00:02:13.933 --> 00:02:16.563
The new Pod passes its readiness check.

45
00:02:16.563 --> 00:02:17.979
Now it gets visitors.

46
00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:20.071
Step two.

47
00:02:20.071 --> 00:02:22.468
Only now may one old Pod go.

48
00:02:22.468 --> 00:02:25.293
The old ReplicaSet scales to two.

49
00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:26.949
Then the same again.

50
00:02:26.949 --> 00:02:31.008
A second new Pod, wait for ready, one more old Pod home.

51
00:02:31.008 --> 00:02:31.877
And a third.

52
00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:35.366
Never fewer than three ready Pods.

53
00:02:35.366 --> 00:02:39.827
The price is speed, and room on some node for one extra Pod at a time.

54
00:02:40.033 --> 00:02:44.288
And notice: in the middle, both versions served visitors at once.

55
00:02:44.288 --> 00:02:47.364
So version two must work alongside version one.

56
00:02:47.567 --> 00:02:49.868
The old ReplicaSet is not deleted.

57
00:02:49.868 --> 00:02:55.623
It stays at zero, like last month's menu in a drawer, and that makes going back fast.

58
00:02:58.133 --> 00:02:59.381
Now the scary one.

59
00:02:59.381 --> 00:03:02.708
Someone ships a typo: a tag that does not exist.

60
00:03:02.708 --> 00:03:03.816
Pause and guess.

61
00:03:03.816 --> 00:03:05.757
What do the visitors notice?

62
00:03:05.757 --> 00:03:08.599
And does Kubernetes roll back on its own?

63
00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:13.623
A third ReplicaSet scales to one.

64
00:03:13.623 --> 00:03:17.073
Its Pod cannot pull, that is download, its image.

65
00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:18.419
ErrImagePull.

66
00:03:18.419 --> 00:03:20.312
Then ImagePullBackOff.

67
00:03:20.312 --> 00:03:22.291
It never becomes ready.

68
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:26.125
And with maxUnavailable at zero, no old Pod may leave.

69
00:03:26.125 --> 00:03:29.482
So the three good Pods keep serving every visitor.

70
00:03:29.482 --> 00:03:31.160
The broken one gets none.

71
00:03:31.367 --> 00:03:33.445
Visitors notice very little.

72
00:03:33.445 --> 00:03:35.374
The rollout simply stalls.

73
00:03:35.374 --> 00:03:38.640
But Kubernetes does not roll back by itself.

74
00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:39.308
It waits.

75
00:03:39.533 --> 00:03:45.172
After six hundred seconds by default, the Deployment reports ProgressDeadlineExceeded.

76
00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:51.238
kubectl rollout status, with a timeout, then fails, so an automatic check can notice.

77
00:03:51.238 --> 00:03:54.053
And you, or that check, run rollout undo.

78
00:03:54.267 --> 00:03:56.955
The broken shift list goes back to zero.

79
00:03:56.955 --> 00:03:58.568
The floor never noticed.

80
00:03:59.500 --> 00:04:02.679
What we just watched has a name: a rolling update.

81
00:04:02.679 --> 00:04:07.702
It replaces old Pods with new ones a few at a time, so the app keeps answering.

82
00:04:07.702 --> 00:04:09.419
It is the default strategy.

83
00:04:09.633 --> 00:04:12.451
But every step waited for one word: ready.

84
00:04:12.451 --> 00:04:13.792
So who decides that?

85
00:04:13.792 --> 00:04:19.360
A readiness check, which the kubelet asks every few seconds: can you take visitors?

86
00:04:19.567 --> 00:04:23.741
Without one, a Pod counts as ready as soon as its container starts.

87
00:04:23.741 --> 00:04:27.729
A new waiter who is ready the moment they walk in, apron or not.

88
00:04:27.933 --> 00:04:33.305
And a version that starts but answers every request with an error is worse than our typo.

89
00:04:33.305 --> 00:04:35.840
It looks ready, and the rollout completes.

90
00:04:36.067 --> 00:04:37.350
So here is the rule.

91
00:04:37.350 --> 00:04:40.880
A Deployment is only as careful as its readiness check.

92
00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:43.511
Add one before you trust rolling updates.

93
00:04:44.533 --> 00:04:46.027
The moral of the story.

94
00:04:46.027 --> 00:04:48.105
One in, wait for ready, one out.

95
00:04:48.105 --> 00:04:50.247
The old menu stays in the drawer.

96
00:04:50.247 --> 00:04:53.819
And a broken version stalls, so you roll back yourself.

97
00:04:54.033 --> 00:04:55.079
One quick check.

98
00:04:55.079 --> 00:04:59.586
Four replicas, with both dials at their default, twenty five percent.

99
00:04:59.586 --> 00:05:03.440
How many extra Pods may exist, and how many may be missing?

100
00:05:06.167 --> 00:05:08.433
One extra, because surge rounds up.

101
00:05:08.433 --> 00:05:11.347
One missing, because unavailable rounds down.

102
00:05:11.347 --> 00:05:15.232
So at least three available Pods, and at most five in total.
